<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Burundi</h1><p class="st-meta">1680 CE – 1966 CE (approx.) · capital Muramvya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Great Lakes kingdom ruled by the Ganwa princes for nearly three centuries, which reached independence as a monarchy before being overthrown in 1966.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-i-of-burundi">Ntare I Rushatsi</a> as founder of Burundi's royal line in the late 17th century, uniting chiefdoms in the hills east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. The kingdom expanded greatly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-iv-rugamba">Ntare IV Rugamba</a> in the early 19th century, absorbing parts of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bugesera">Kingdom of Bugesera</a> and other neighbours.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The mwami (king) was a sacred ruler, but much power lay with the ganwa, princes of royal blood who governed provinces and often fought each other. Society included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> farmers, Tutsi herders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes-twa">Twa</a>, with royal rituals such as the annual muganuro <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> festival. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwezi-iv-gisabo">Mwezi IV Gisabo</a> resisted German forces until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiganda">Treaty of Kiganda</a> in 1903.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Burundi became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> and then <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ruanda-urundi">Belgian Ruanda-Urundi</a>. It gained independence in 1962 as a kingdom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwambutsa-iv">Mwambutsa IV</a>, but political violence followed, and in 1966 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-v-of-burundi">Ntare V</a> and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-burundi">Republic of Burundi</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-burundi.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Burundi" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burundi_(1962%E2%80%931966).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1680 CE – 1966 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Muramvya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ntare IV Rugamba, Mwezi IV Gisabo, Mwambutsa IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, later Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kirundi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Burundi, Urundi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1823&amp;focus=kingdom-of-burundi" aria-label="Kingdom of Burundi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5507 2315 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Burundi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5827 2571L6087 2688M5827 2571L5830 2535M5827 2571L5831 2562"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6087 2688h0M5830 2535h0M5831 2562h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5827 2571h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5827 2571h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1823&amp;focus=kingdom-of-burundi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
